1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,220
My neighbour leaned over the fence and said:

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,260
"You pay for private health insurance,

3
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,220
and I got the exact same top surgeon

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,180
for free!

5
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,820
What's the point?" Fair question!

6
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,740
Let's look behind the scenes.

7
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:13,900
We're talking about planned surgery,

8
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:16,540
called elective surgery, like a new knee or

9
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:17,500
a new hip.

10
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:19,900
Something you know about ahead of time.

11
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,500
In a public hospital,

12
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:24,380
the hospital chooses who does your operation.

13
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,860
My neighbour happened to get the top surgeon.

14
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:28,860
That was luck, not a promise.

15
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,500
So how does one surgeon work in both

16
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:32,620
places?

17
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:33,900
Think of a DJ.

18
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:36,300
Some DJs work for the community hall and

19
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:37,980
get paid a regular wage.

20
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,060
Others are hired by the hall to play

21
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:41,820
a set number of shows.

22
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,180
Lots of them do both:

23
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:44,180
shows at the hall,

24
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,060
and private parties too.

25
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:47,940
The hall gives them steady work,

26
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:49,740
great equipment, tricky gigs,

27
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:51,660
and new DJs to teach.

28
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,980
Private parties often pay more.

29
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,100
Surgeons work the same way.

30
00:00:56,160 --> 00:00:58,780
Big public hospitals are also where new surgeons

31
00:00:58,800 --> 00:00:59,580
learn.

32
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,700
Your operation might be done by a doctor

33
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:03,900
still in training, with the top surgeon guiding

34
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:04,580
them.

35
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:06,060
You're not a guinea pig.

36
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:08,700
Every great surgeon had to learn somewhere.

37
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:09,980
As a private patient,

38
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,300
you choose your surgeon,

39
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:13,580
and your surgeon does your operation.

40
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,980
Doctors in training only join in if you

41
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:17,220
say yes.

42
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:18,780
So how long do you wait in the

43
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,220
public system?

44
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:21,980
Honestly, it depends:

45
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,620
where you live, which hospital covers your area,

46
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:26,660
how many people need that specialist,

47
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:28,940
and how serious your problem is.

48
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:31,340
First you wait just to see the specialist.

49
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:32,780
That can take months,

50
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:34,140
sometimes even years.

51
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,940
Then you wait again for the operation.

52
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:38,940
Take a knee or hip replacement.

53
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:41,300
In the right place at the right time,

54
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:43,580
someone might wait around six months.

55
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:46,300
Somewhere else, people wait years.

56
00:01:46,480 --> 00:01:48,940
That's real life for lots of Australians.

57
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:50,300
And even on the day,

58
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:51,900
it's not guaranteed.

59
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:53,180
You can be in the bed,

60
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:54,820
gown on, ready to go,

61
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:56,540
and an emergency comes in.

62
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:58,100
The surgeon has to go,

63
00:01:58,120 --> 00:01:59,980
and your operation is cancelled.

64
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:02,860
Going private, seeing the surgeon is often weeks

65
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:05,580
away, and you plan the operation date together.

66
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:08,860
Most private hospitals don't have an emergency department,

67
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:10,940
so your operation is much less likely to

68
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:11,740
be bumped.

69
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:14,300
So yes, we had the same surgeon.

70
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:15,420
But I chose him,

71
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:16,780
he did my operation,

72
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:17,820
I picked the date,

73
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:19,660
and it happened on that date.

74
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:22,140
My neighbour waited, and got lucky.

75
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:24,647
But she had one more question:

76
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:26,700
"An ambulance usually takes you to the public

77
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:28,540
emergency department anyway.

78
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:30,140
So if it's an emergency,

79
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:33,500
why have private insurance at all?" Great question.

80
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:36,660
Stay tuned, because that's our next video!
